<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <AnalysisLevel>latest-recommended</AnalysisLevel>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!-- CA1848/CA1873: LoggerMessage-delegate perf rules — noise at this scale -->
    <!-- CA1716 ("Shared" namespace keyword clash): library-author rule, app is not a consumed library -->
    <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);CA1848;CA1873;CA1716</NoWarn>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <!--
    SAML sign-in (#139) brings ITfoxtec.Identity.Saml2, which declares a transitive dependency on
    System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 10.0.7. That version carries five high-severity advisories in
    XML signature handling — the code SAML trusts to tell a real assertion from a forged one — so
    the audit failing the build here was the right call and worth reading twice.

    It is suppressed rather than fixed with a version pin because that assembly ships *in the .NET
    shared framework*: NuGet prunes the package reference entirely (NU1510 refuses an explicit
    one), so the 10.0.7 package is never restored, never deployed, and never loaded. What actually
    executes is whatever System.Security.Cryptography.Xml the installed runtime provides.

    Which means the real mitigation is a patched runtime, not a line in a csproj. That is a
    deployment obligation, and it is written down in docs/saml-sso.md rather than left implicit
    here. Re-check this block whenever ITfoxtec is upgraded: if it ever stops being prunable, the
    suppression stops being honest and has to become a pin.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-23rf-6693-g89p" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-8q5v-6pqq-x66h" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-cvvh-rhrc-wg4q" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-g8r8-53c2-pm3f" />
    <NuGetAuditSuppress Include="https://github.com/advisories/GHSA-mmjf-rqrv-855v" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
